iio: adc: sophgo-saradc: arm the completion per conversion

The driver keeps one struct completion for the whole device and never
resets it between reads, so the completion's done counter is free to
drift above zero. Every surplus complete() makes a later
wait_for_completion_timeout() return without an interrupt having been
raised for the conversion it is actually waiting on, and read_raw()
then reads CH_RESULT_REG while the SAR is still converting. The
CH_VALID bit does not catch this: it is still set from the previous
conversion on that channel.

There are three ways the counter drifts:

 1. The interrupt is level triggered (GIC SPI 116 on the SG2000) and
    the INTR_CLR write in the handler is posted, so the line can still
    be asserted when the handler returns. The GIC re-presents it, the
    handler runs a second time for one conversion, and completes twice.

 2. A wait that times out leaves its interrupt to arrive afterwards,
    and nothing consumes that completion.

 3. probe() enables the interrupt without clearing INTR_STA. The FSBL
    and u-boot both read the SARADC on this SoC, so the status is
    frequently already latched and the handler fires once before the
    first read ever happens.

Fix all three: read INTR_STA back to flush the posted clear, clear a
stale status before enabling the interrupt, and reinit_completion()
ahead of each measurement so a wait always requires an interrupt from
that conversion.

Measured on a Milk-V Duo S (SG2000) reading the 5 V rail through a
2.2M/1M divider. The median is correct either way; what changes is the
proportion of individual samples that are junk. Sampling as fast as
sysfs allows, with nothing else touching the ADC:

  before: 69% of samples more than 15% off the median (min 130, max 704)
  after:   4% (min 272, max 384)

At one sample every 50 ms it goes from 46% to 0%. The median is 320 either
way, which is the correct 4.960 V through this board divider -- only the
spread changes.

Note this leaves a separate race in the polled path (irq < 0)
untouched: readl_poll_timeout() evaluates its condition before its
first delay, so it can observe BUSY still clear from before the
conversion started and return immediately. The SG2000 wires up the
interrupt, so that path is not exercised here and the fix is left to
someone who can test it.

Fixes: d1022ff5f9b9 ("iio: adc: sophgo-saradc: Add driver for Sophgo CV1800B SARADC")
Signed-off-by: Morgan Jones <me@numin.it>
---
--- a/drivers/iio/adc/sophgo-cv1800b-adc.c
+++ b/drivers/iio/adc/sophgo-cv1800b-adc.c
@@ -71,6 +71,19 @@
 static void cv1800b_adc_start_measurement(struct cv1800b_adc *saradc,
 					  int channel)
 {
+	/*
+	 * One completion is shared by every read, and nothing resets it
+	 * between conversions, so any surplus complete() -- a duplicate from
+	 * the level-triggered handler, or one that lands after a wait timed
+	 * out -- leaves the counter non-zero. The next
+	 * wait_for_completion_timeout() then returns immediately and the
+	 * result register is read while a conversion is still in flight,
+	 * yielding a partial SAR value. Arm it fresh for each measurement so
+	 * a wait always requires an interrupt raised by THIS conversion.
+	 */
+	if (saradc->irq > 0)
+		reinit_completion(&saradc->completion);
+
 	writel(0, saradc->regs + CV1800B_ADC_CTRL_REG);
 	writel(CV1800B_ADC_SEL(channel) | CV1800B_ADC_EN,
 	       saradc->regs + CV1800B_ADC_CTRL_REG);
@@ -146,6 +159,14 @@
 		return IRQ_NONE;
 
 	writel(CV1800B_ADC_INTR_CLR_BIT, saradc->regs + CV1800B_ADC_INTR_CLR_REG);
+	/*
+	 * The interrupt is level triggered and the clear above is a posted
+	 * write. Read the status register back so the clear has reached the
+	 * device before this handler returns; otherwise the line can still be
+	 * asserted at the GIC, the handler re-runs, and a single conversion
+	 * completes the completion twice.
+	 */
+	readl(saradc->regs + CV1800B_ADC_INTR_STA_REG);
 	complete(&saradc->completion);
 
 	return IRQ_HANDLED;
@@ -183,6 +204,15 @@
 
 	saradc->irq = platform_get_irq_optional(pdev, 0);
 	if (saradc->irq > 0) {
+		/*
+		 * The FSBL and u-boot both take SARADC readings, so a
+		 * conversion-complete status can already be latched here.
+		 * Enabling the interrupt over it fires the handler before the
+		 * first read and pre-charges the completion. Clear it first.
+		 */
+		writel(CV1800B_ADC_INTR_CLR_BIT,
+		       saradc->regs + CV1800B_ADC_INTR_CLR_REG);
+
 		init_completion(&saradc->completion);
 		ret = devm_request_irq(dev, saradc->irq,
 				       cv1800b_adc_interrupt_handler, 0,
